  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 242 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nBy Friday night, the pressure gradient weakens as an Arctic high \nslides into the Midwest, leading to light surface winds and a mostly \nclear sky. This synoptic setup, with the surface high just to the \nnorth providing calm/light northerly flow, will allow for efficient \nradiational cooling across the entire area. As a result, Saturday \nmorning will feature the coldest air of the season with a widespread \nhard freeze expected as lows reach the 20s across most the area. \nSome low-lying sheltered locations may lower into the upper teens in \nlocalized instances. The slow moving surface high will maintain \nclose influence on the area through the weekend allowing both \nSaturday and Sunday night to drop below freezing across most of the \nregion. Highs will gradually moderate Saturday into Sunday thanks to \na slight airmass modification as 850mb temperatures warm to around \n1-2C, allowing temperatures to reach near 50 by Sunday afternoon.\n\nMid-level ridging slides into the region from the Great Plains by \nTuesday with the surface high sliding east as well, bringing a \nreturn to southerly WAA. This will allow for a gradual warming trend \nfrom Tuesday to next Thursday as LREF mean 850mb temperatures reach \n>5C by the middle of next week. Assuming full mixing, that would \ncorrelate to highs in the upper 50s/low 60s in the afternoon. Light \nsurface winds are forecast each night until Wednesday night, meaning \nthat if there is a lack of cloud cover, most rural areas have the \npotential to fall below freezing each morning despite a gradual \nwarming of high temperatures.\n\nMostly dry conditions are forecast to continue through at least the \nmiddle of the week with good model consensus in long-range guidance. \nThe LREF has <10% chances for measurable precipitation until at \nleast the middle part of next week. By next Thursday, rain chances \nreturn as guidance suggests the return of western longwave troughing \nthat would favor moist southerly flow back into the region.",
